# Findable identity text

Shared primitive: [components/tfl/findable-text.tsx](../../components/tfl/findable-text.tsx). Canonical consumer: [components/tfl/station-name.tsx](../../components/tfl/station-name.tsx).

## The rule

If a component ever paints **characters that differ from the canonical name** — real abbreviation (`St` for `Street`), or a name split across a `<br>` — wrap the visible node in `FindableText`, or compose `StationName`, which already does this for station names.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — visible paint differs from canonical text (abbreviated / <br>-split)
<FindableText text={fullLineName} paintMatchesText={false}>
  <span>{abbreviatedLineName}</span>
</FindableText>
```

`enableFind` defaults to **on**. If a caller disables it, say why in a comment — don't disable it to "simplify" a diff.

## When you do *not* need this

Plain CSS clipping — `truncate`, `line-clamp-*`, `overflow-hidden` + `text-overflow: ellipsis`, `whitespace-nowrap` — never touches the underlying DOM text. The browser already finds and copies the full string through the visual clip; `Ctrl+F` and `Ctrl+C` work correctly without any wrapper.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — CSS-only clipping, nothing to wrap
<span className="truncate">{stop.name}</span>

// ❌ BAD — defensive wrapping that fixes nothing and adds markup
<FindableText text={stop.name}>
  <span className="truncate">{stop.name}</span>
</FindableText>
```

Do not retrofit `FindableText` onto surfaces using only CSS clipping — it's dead weight and conflicts with this repo's "keep changes small" convention (see [project.mdc](project.mdc)).

## Why this exists

Chrome's find-in-page cannot match a phrase across a `<br>`, and abbreviated characters are gone from the DOM outright — no CSS trick fixes either. `FindableText` exposes the canonical string via `hidden="until-found"` only when the current find query would not already highlight the painted copy (searching `Liverpool St` on a wrapped `Liverpool Street` must not also reveal the chip). It re-hides a beat after a match (there's no native "find closed" event, so it restarts the countdown if the browser matches it again). See the component's own comments for the underlying constraints (React's boolean `hidden` typing, `until-found` requiring non-inline `display`, Firefox/Safari fallback).

## Anti-patterns

- Wrapping a plain `truncate`/`nowrap` label in `FindableText` "for consistency" when the DOM text is already the full string
- Rolling a second `hidden="until-found"` implementation per component instead of composing the shared primitive
- Disabling `enableFind` without a comment explaining why
- Splitting a name across `<br>` or substituting abbreviated characters without wrapping in `FindableText`
